{"name":"Genetic Code","hex":"#18515d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"genetic code","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The set of rules by which the sequence of bases in DNA are translated into the amino acid sequence of proteins."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/genetic_code"}],"entries":[{"title":"Genetic code","blocks":[{"text":"Genetic code is a set of rules used by living cells to translate information encoded within genetic material into proteins. Translation is accomplished by the ribosome, which links proteinogenic amino acids in an order specified by messenger RNA (mRNA), using transfer RNA (tRNA) molecules to carry amino acids and to read the mRNA three nucleotides at a time. The genetic code is highly similar among all organisms and can be expressed in a simple table with 64 entries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/RNA-codons.svg/330px-RNA-codons.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genetic_code","wikidata":"Q180618"},{"title":"Code","blocks":[{"text":"In communications and information processing, a code is a system of rules to convert information—such as a letter, word, sound, image, or gesture—into another form, sometimes shortened or secret, for communication through a communication channel or storage in a storage medium."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code","wikidata":"Q188889"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Codon.png/500px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Codon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Codon.png/1280px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Codon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Codon.png/500px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Codon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A triplet of nucleotides that can be read by the ribosome to specify an amino acid or the start or stop of a polypeptide.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AXBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Codon.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Genetic_Code_Simple_Corrected.pdf/page1-500px-Genetic_Code_Simple_Corrected.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Genetic_Code_Simple_Corrected.pdf/page1-1280px-Genetic_Code_Simple_Corrected.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Genetic_Code_Simple_Corrected.pdf/page1-500px-Genetic_Code_Simple_Corrected.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Grouping of codons by amino acid residue molar volume and hydropathicity.  A more detailed version is available.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGenetic_Code_Simple_Corrected.pdf"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/3D_Genetic_Code.jpg/500px-3D_Genetic_Code.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/3D_Genetic_Code.jpg/1280px-3D_Genetic_Code.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/3D_Genetic_Code.jpg/500px-3D_Genetic_Code.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Axes 1, 2, 3 are the first, second, and third positions in the codon. The 20 amino acids and stop codons (X) are shown in single letter code.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A3D_Genetic_Code.jpg"}